@anon.863986d00e4103fb69f29fe2b65b0919.756 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ [2792 x i8] }> <{ [2792 x i8] c"A table of items.\0A\0ATables are used to arrange content in cells. Cells can contain arbitrary\0Acontent, including multiple paragraphs and are specified in row-major order.\0AFor a hands-on explanation of all the ways you can use and customize tables\0Ain Typst, check out the [table guide]($guides/table-guide).\0A\0ABecause tables are just grids with different defaults for some cell\0Aproperties (notably `stroke` and `inset`), refer to the [grid\0Adocumentation]($grid) for more information on how to size the table tracks\0Aand specify the cell appearance properties.\0A\0AIf you are unsure whether you should be using a table or a grid, consider\0Awhether the content you are arranging semantically belongs together as a set\0Aof related data points or similar or whether you are just want to enhance\0Ayour presentation by arranging unrelated content in a grid. In the former\0Acase, a table is the right choice, while in the latter case, a grid is more\0Aappropriate. Furthermore, Typst will annotate its output in the future such\0Athat screenreaders will annouce content in `table` as tabular while a grid's\0Acontent will be announced no different than multiple content blocks in the\0Adocument flow.\0A\0ANote that, to override a particular cell's properties or apply show rules on\0Atable cells, you can use the [`table.cell`]($table.cell) element. See its\0Adocumentation for more information.\0A\0AAlthough the `table` and the `grid` share most properties, set and show\0Arules on one of them do not affect the other.\0A\0ATo give a table a caption and make it [referenceable]($ref), put it into a\0A[figure].\0A\0A# Example\0A\0AThe example below demonstrates some of the most common table options.\0A```example\0A#table(\0A  columns: (1fr, auto, auto),\0A  inset: 10pt,\0A  align: horizon,\0A  table.header(\0A    [], [*Area*], [*Parameters*],\0A  ),\0A  image(\22cylinder.svg\22),\0A  $ pi h (D^2 - d^2) / 4 $,\0A  [\0A    $h$: height \\\0A    $D$: outer radius \\\0A    $d$: inner radius\0A  ],\0A  image(\22tetrahedron.svg\22),\0A  $ sqrt(2) / 12 a^3 $,\0A  [$a$: edge length]\0A)\0A```\0A\0AMuch like with grids, you can use [`table.cell`]($table.cell) to customize\0Athe appearance and the position of each cell.\0A\0A```example\0A>>> #set page(width: auto)\0A>>> #set text(font: \22IBM Plex Sans\22)\0A>>> #let gray = rgb(\22#565565\22)\0A>>>\0A#set table(\0A  stroke: none,\0A  gutter: 0.2em,\0A  fill: (x, y) =>\0A    if x == 0 or y == 0 { gray },\0A  inset: (right: 1.5em),\0A)\0A\0A#show table.cell: it => {\0A  if it.x == 0 or it.y == 0 {\0A    set text(white)\0A    strong(it)\0A  } else if it.body == [] {\0A    // Replace empty cells with 'N/A'\0A    pad(..it.inset)[_N/A_]\0A  } else {\0A    it\0A  }\0A}\0A\0A#let a = table.cell(\0A  fill: green.lighten(60%),\0A)[A]\0A#let b = table.cell(\0A  fill: aqua.lighten(60%),\0A)[B]\0A\0A#table(\0A  columns: 4,\0A  [], [Exam 1], [Exam 2], [Exam 3],\0A\0A  [John], [], a, [],\0A  [Mary], [], a, a,\0A  [Robert], b, a, b,\0A)\0A```" }>, align 1
